<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The rule is https everywhere, except plain http to the viewer's own machine or their own network.
  A session's bearer and refresh tokens travel on every call, so on a network the viewer does not
  own they must be encrypted.

  This file cannot state that rule. It names hosts one at a time and has no way to say "anything in
  127/8, 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16 or 169.254/16", which is where a self hosted server sits: the
  launcher's public flag picks this machine's address on the network it would use to leave and
  prints it, and the README offers that address as the way to reach the server from another device.
  The list this file used to carry, of localhost, 127.0.0.1, the emulator's host and the mDNS
  names, refused every bare private address, and it refused them for the whole app: the calls, the
  artwork and the video all ask this policy first. So a self hoster who started the server on their
  network and typed the 192.168 address it had just printed got an IOException the app could only
  report as being offline.

  So the rule is kept in the one place that can state it. Host.connect() is the gate every server
  address passes, typed or kept: it allows https anywhere, allows plain http only to loopback, a
  private range or an mDNS name, and refuses anything further out before a call is ever made. Every
  address the app then uses is built from that server. The platform no longer holds a narrower copy
  of the same rule that contradicts it.

  What this does give up, and it is the whole of it: an absolute http address a server hands over
  for artwork on some other host now loads instead of failing. Such a call carries no session, by
  the contract: images go straight from their host to the viewer.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
